00:03:15.558 --> 00:03:18.122
So the design goals-- we
had four basic design goals

00:03:18.122 --> 00:03:19.330
when we started this project.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.790
The first was we wanted
to be able to leverage

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.720
the cloud and the real world.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.260
So we want to be able
to take the cloud

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.830
and use the cloud for
complex training--

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.610
intensive and complex--
but we also want

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:35.890
to be able to deploy
this in the real world,

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:42.160
actually onto robots
themselves, not just on a game

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.630
on the internet or something.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.060
The next is we want to
leverage existing tools.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.180
So great things are built
on the shoulders of giants.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.800
We wanted to be able
to leverage those tools

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.760
and so we can move quickly
and get results quickly.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.320
We also wanted to use as
much automation as possible

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.660
so that we can sort of
lower the cost of training,

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.990
which is one of the most
significant costs of machine

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.054
learning.

00:04:06.054 --> 00:04:07.720
And then finally, we
wanted to have fun.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.050
We wanted this to be as much
fun as it was educational.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:15.610
And that's why we created
these robotic games.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:19.600
The key architecture decisions
we made through the process--

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.950
first, I mentioned
earlier, cloud training.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:25.590
So we wanted to be able to use
the cloud for rapid iteration

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.840
in order to improve
our models quickly,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:32.721
to be able to lower the cost of
using the latest cutting-edge

00:04:32.721 --> 00:04:33.220
hardware--

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.880
TPUs and GPUs, for instance,
and to really only pay

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.620
for what we use.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.110
The second architectural
decision we made was we

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.200
wanted to make sure that
we had focus on the models

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.670
or optimization of the models.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.590
So what that meant
ultimately was

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:49.930
we ended up with two
separate models-- one model

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:54.550
to be able to see and
understand the world, which

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.940
Noah will walk through in a
minute, for object detection.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.160
And the second is
we wanted to have

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.190
a model that gave the right path
for direction for the gameplay.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:08.620
And that ended up-- we
called it a commander model.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:09.940
NOAH NEGREY: So Dave, we
