00:05:11.398 --> 00:05:13.990
So why are we focusing
on-device prediction?

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.810
DAVE ELLIOTT: So
it's a good question.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:21.550
But to me, I think it's because
of the emerging use cases that

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:27.640
are really interesting, the
disconnected world of devices,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.140
like a warehouse and you're
doing inventory management.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:34.480
Or a manufacturing
facility, a plant,

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.330
you want to look at defects
in what's being built,

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.530
or security.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.570
So you've got these
the disconnected,

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:43.660
not-always-on
devices, and you want

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.270
to be able to do the
prediction in realtime

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.040
with these disconnected devices.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.460
So low latency, disconnected,
and predictions,

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.876
to mimic the real world.

00:05:53.876 --> 00:05:56.000
The hardware decisions were
pretty straightforward.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.330
We ended up looking at a bunch
of really inexpensive and fun

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:58.830
robots.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.860
We selected the Sphero robots.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.940
They're really
easy to work with,

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.300
a very simple set of APIs.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.980
And we also used the
Pixel 2 and the 2XL.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.380
And I mentioned the
Huawei P20 phone.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.510
Really, it could have
been any Android devices.

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.280
These are just ones
that were easily

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.440
available and easy to work with.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.790
The machine learning process--

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:26.000
this is where I think Noah will
walk through the second half

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.175
of this webinar.

00:06:27.175 --> 00:06:29.050
If you're not familiar
with machine learning,

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:34.430
I highly recommend taking a look
at "AI Adventures" from Yufeng

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.030
Guo, "The 7 Steps of
Machine Learning."

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.350
I've summarized it here--

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.160
the process we went through--

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.330
to four steps.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.340
We first gathered and
prepared the data.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.580
Remember, machine learning is
really about using examples--

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.210
or in this case,
examples are the data--

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.860
in order to make predictions.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.500
So we simulated some data.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.090
And Noah will walk
through that again.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:56.964
Choosing a model-- and
as I mentioned before,

00:06:56.964 --> 00:07:00.070
we ended up with two models,
one for object detection,

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:01.000
and one for--

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.750
the commander for making
decisions on directions.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:06.280
The third step is
really the heart

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:07.821
of it-- the training,
the evaluation,

00:07:07.821 --> 00:07:09.807
and the tuning of the model.

00:07:09.807 --> 00:07:11.640
And then finally, this
on-device prediction,

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.790
actually being able to use these
inexpensive, easily available,

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:19.350
disconnected devices
to make predictions.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.170
So this is robot games.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.880
And we looked at a
lot of different games

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.200
that we could have played, and
we settled on these four games.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:36.210
And they really are sort of
a snapshot of chaser games.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:39.090
We created these four
games in particular.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.680
The first was Bot Freeze Tag.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.290
And this is where,
working together,

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:46.680
humans compete in a
game of freeze tag

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.570
against an individual AI robot.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:54.390
An individual robotic-controlled
robot would touch each player,

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.369
freeze them, and
individual humans

00:07:56.369 --> 00:07:58.410
would have to run around
and unfreeze each other.

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:01.680
Then we switched it around, and
we created Human Freeze Tag,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.900
where there was an
individual human trying

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.530
to freeze the
AI-controlled robots,

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.820
and AI-controlled robots
would unfreeze each other.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.360
The third one is what we
called Zombie Apocalypse.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.310
And this was a little
bit like the freeze tag,

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.380
except in this case,
it was-- actually,

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.060
this is one of the
most popular ones--

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.705
when you're tagged,
the human becomes

00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:30.930
a part of the model
that chases or pursues

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.309
the other human players.

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:34.440
And then finally we
played a traditional game

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.940
of Capture the Flag.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:38.490
And you scored based
upon how quickly you're

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:39.890
able to capture the other flag.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.950
And I'm running over just
designated points in the game.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:45.930
OK, so those were the
architecture decisions

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.149
we made, the game design.

00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:50.940
And at this stage, I'm
going to had it over

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.550
to Noah, who's going to
walk through model selection

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:54.510
and training.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:55.110
NOAH NEGREY: All right, sweet.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:55.710
Let's jump in.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.660
So as Dave was
saying, the first step

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.384
is data gathering and
preparation process.

00:09:00.384 --> 00:09:01.800
And so the key
thing for us is, we

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.550
didn't have a large data set
already of Sphero robots,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.290
with images and labels.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.990
So we needed to
generate that data set.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:10.740
And going through, we
could do it by hand,

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.620
taking pictures, going back,
manually labeling them.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:14.800
But that would take forever.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.660
So we wanted to
generate our data set.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.618
And so what we have here
is, on that top image,

00:09:18.618 --> 00:09:21.930
this blank canvas image
of our arena floor,

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.010
and then a cropped
image on the right.

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.430
And so we start placing these
randomly around the image.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.170
What you see on the bottom is
these randomly placed objects.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.440
And when we randomly place
them with a simple Python

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.009
script using the
Pillow library, we also

00:09:34.009 --> 00:09:35.550
get the coordinates
of where they are

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:36.870
and the type that we placed.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:41.380
So this is sort of our
feature and label data set.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:43.860
And so then, now that we've
generated this data set,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.010
we can generate
like 1,200 images

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.720
and convert it to a
TensorFlow record.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.780
And so the idea behind
that is that TensorFlow

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.690
uses these TF records to
quickly access data-- as well

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.660
as the labels during training--
to help speed up the process.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:59.910
So we'll talk about the
conversion script a little bit

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:01.560
later, and how we used it.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.280
But after that, we upload our
data up to Google Cloud Storage

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:06.280
so that we can store it.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.100
Because this is 1,200 images--

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.632
large, several gigabytes.

00:10:09.632 --> 00:10:11.590
And so we need to be able
to access it quickly.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:13.604
And if we want to run
multiple models at once,

00:10:13.604 --> 00:10:15.270
we want to make sure
they can all access

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:16.500
that data at the same time.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.458
DAVE ELLIOTT: So two
things I'll say that that.

00:10:18.458 --> 00:10:20.950
One is-- this goes back to
what I've mentioned before--

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:22.260
we want to leverage automation.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:23.301
And so these are virtual.

00:10:23.301 --> 00:10:26.570
The 1,200 items
that Noah mentioned,

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:27.840
these are virtual items.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:28.923
It could have been 12,000.

00:10:28.923 --> 00:10:30.069
It could have been 120,000.

00:10:30.069 --> 00:10:32.610
So we were trying to leverage
automation as much as possible.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.734
And the second thing is,
it might make sense for us

00:10:34.734 --> 00:10:37.320
to explain what TensorFlow
is since TensorFlow

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.572
is at the heart of
what we used here.

00:10:39.572 --> 00:10:42.030
NOAH NEGREY: So TensorFlow is
a machine learning framework.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.280
So there's a bunch of different
frameworks out there--

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.020
Scikit-learn, PyTorch--
but TensorFlow

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:49.080
is what we used, also because
of TensorFlow Lite, which we'll

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.660
dig into later,
that has a great use

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.100
for embedded and mobile devices.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:58.320
So that's a sort of a library
in Python and other languages--

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:01.884
Node.js [INAUDIBLE] JavaScript.

00:11:01.884 --> 00:11:02.800
But let's jump in now.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:03.880
So we're going to be
talking about the two

