00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.370
So when we first started,
we kind of looked around,

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.650
leveraging out the
resources we had,

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:26.290
to see what things out
there that we could use.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.450
And right from the
beginning, TensorFlow

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.970
has, on their GitHub,
simple Android applications

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:35.380
that go and provide, basically,
a simple app that already does

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.570
object detection
with a model in there

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:40.870
to do keyboard recognition,
general person recognition,

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:43.030
maybe like water bottle
recognition, very

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:45.940
simple things, and shows you how
to run TensorFlow on an Android

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:46.630
app.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.520
So we grabbed that off
of their GitHub repo,

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:49.680
and just started with that.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.360
But the problem with that was
we didn't get our custom sort

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.190
of prediction of
our Sphero robots

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.350
from that data set
that we generated.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.320
So then we had to decide, hey,
we need a custom sort of object

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:00.820
detection model, and that--

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:01.480
DAVE ELLIOTT: You're saying
that you have millions

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:03.580
of pictures of Sphero robots--

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:03.910
NOAH NEGREY: No, they didn't--

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:04.600
DAVE ELLIOTT:
--already in the model.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:05.850
NOAH NEGREY: Oddly enough, no.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.630
So we had to build
our own model.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:10.470
And so but the nice thing
was, we could take--

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.270
TensorFlow has this thing, what
they call the Object Detection

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:13.812
API.

00:12:13.812 --> 00:12:16.270
And that's going to be that
bottom resource, the MobileNets

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:20.680
Open-Source Models for
Efficient Vision On-Device.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.420
And so what that is
is basically there's

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:25.720
these pre-trained models,
frozen at checkpoints, that do

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:27.470
object detection very well.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:30.460
But what they don't have is
very specific object detection.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:33.380
And so we can take our
data set with that model,

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.590
run it through training
by modifying just some

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:38.290
of the configuration files,
and get a brand new model

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.680
out that is now trained to do
object detection on our models.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.765
And the code for that
can sound intimidating.

00:12:43.765 --> 00:12:44.740
You're like whoa,
I don't know how

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:45.820
to do this object detection.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:47.194
But let's look at
the next slide,

00:12:47.194 --> 00:12:50.020
and just see sort of
what we have to modify.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.140
And so what we take here is we
see, modifying just a few lines

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:57.850
in the configuration file of
the input path and the label map

00:12:57.850 --> 00:12:58.750
path.

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:00.550
And what's happening
there is we just

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:03.040
are modifying that
TF record that we

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:05.770
uploaded to Google Cloud
Storage, and adding those in.

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:09.610
And then we run the scripts that
we got as part of this model

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:10.720
to run the training job.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:15.727
So next, let's talk about
some of those decisions

00:13:15.727 --> 00:13:17.060
we made for the commander model.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.676
That's the reacting model.

00:13:18.676 --> 00:13:20.050
And so the idea
behind this model

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.800
is we've got the bounding boxes
that you see in that image

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:23.300
there.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:26.120
We've figured out where
things are on the image.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.580
We've understood
our environment.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:29.830
But now let's make a decision.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:31.496
And that's where this
model's coming in,

00:13:31.496 --> 00:13:33.470
more built from scratch,
to make a decision

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:35.600
on what the model should do.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.304
And so this model is using
reinforcement learning.

00:13:38.304 --> 00:13:40.220
And this is a technique
where you're basically

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.260
playing a bunch of
games over and over,

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.269
and then giving it a scoring
system to see how well it did,

00:13:45.269 --> 00:13:47.060
and just learning from
that scoring system.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.435
Because we don't
really know what

00:13:48.435 --> 00:13:51.230
the optimal path is
in every situation

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:55.250
when the balls are actually
out there moving around.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:58.632
And so that's how we
used the commander model.

00:13:58.632 --> 00:14:00.590
DAVE ELLIOTT: So we've
got the object detection

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:04.039
to see the world, to understand
the world, these objects

00:14:04.039 --> 00:14:05.580
and the obstacles
that are out there.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:06.920
And then commander
model is the one

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:08.670
that actually plays
the game, the one that

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.670
knows the rules of
the game and makes

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:13.070
the determination as to
what the right path is

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:14.540
in order for the [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:15.490
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah,
well, not quite.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.698
It doesn't really need to
know the rules of the game.

00:14:17.698 --> 00:14:19.910
It just needs to know--

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:22.280
we can teach it the
rules of the game

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:25.560
later, with reinforcement
learning, to play using just--

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.726
DAVE ELLIOTT: It doesn't
know the rules of the game,

00:14:27.726 --> 00:14:29.312
but it knows what's
a successful path.

00:14:29.312 --> 00:14:30.020
NOAH NEGREY: Yes.

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.120
We give it back that
scoring while training.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.520
We're telling it what
the correct path is.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:40.770
So now the next part is
actually training this model,

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:44.110
and running it, and learning
from it, and evaluating.

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.540
And so the key
thing to that is we

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.250
can take advantage
of cloud resources,

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:49.830
using Google Cloud Machine
Learning Engine for training

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.270
of both models.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:52.771
And so the idea
behind this is, when

00:14:52.771 --> 00:14:54.270
we're training these
models, we need

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:55.620
a lot of compute resources.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.780
And we also have
to access the data.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:59.850
And so this is the nice
thing that Google offers,

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.370
is we have all these
resources and compute.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:05.940
We can run many simulations
at once, many training jobs,

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:09.930
and be able to access that data
out of the TensorFlow record.

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:11.670
And so the key thing
to this is there

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.900
are simple one-time setups
for this cloud training stuff.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.274
And you can follow the
links to those GitHubs.

00:15:17.274 --> 00:15:19.440
DAVE ELLIOTT: About how
long did it take to do this?

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.065
NOAH NEGREY: The
simple one-time setup?

00:15:21.065 --> 00:15:22.940
Oh, 15, maybe 20 minutes.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:25.160
And we'll talk in the next
slide about a resource we

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:26.840
used that basically
tells you how

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:30.230
to do this whole process with
a slightly more specific model,

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:32.110
in about 30 minutes,
for object detection.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:33.110
DAVE ELLIOTT: OK, great.

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:35.880
NOAH NEGREY: So
let's look at that.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:38.210
We've got our sample
application we have

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.370
that we just need
to get a model for.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:43.580
We're going to train our
custom object detection model.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:48.080
And now we just need to sort
of start that training job.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.810
And so there's actually
three amazing resources

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:52.490
out there, these
three blog posts--

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:55.330
Sara Robinson is the author
of the first and third one,

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.050
Dat Tran is the
author of the second--

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.120
that basically walk
through these exact steps

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:02.450
with very simple
specific resources

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:03.716
on doing object detection.

00:16:03.716 --> 00:16:05.090
As you can see on
the bottom one,

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:06.930
it's getting started
in 30 minutes.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.770
So you're up and running
with a mobile application,

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:11.460
doing object detection
on your phone

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:13.640
in 30 minutes with a
cloud training job,

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:14.750
using your cloud TPUs--

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:15.562
kind of amazing.

00:16:15.562 --> 00:16:17.270
DAVE ELLIOTT: And this
is another example

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:21.770
of using resources that
exist in the TensorFlow

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:23.330
community and the
broader community

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:27.490
to be able to shorten the
time to get to actually

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:29.240
the gameplay itself.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:30.750
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah, definitely.

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:34.010
So then what we talked about is
our second model, the commander

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:35.534
model, being run in simulation.

00:16:35.534 --> 00:16:37.700
So what you see right there
is our actual simulation

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:38.870
happening.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:40.905
So our model is
using simulations

00:16:40.905 --> 00:16:43.790
so that we can run it faster,
run multiple training jobs.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:44.850
It's less expensive.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:46.850
We don't need to actually
run multiple games

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.790
in the physical environment,
keep logging the data,

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.777
and then go back
and sort of upload

00:16:51.777 --> 00:16:52.860
that, and train from that.

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:54.920
No, we can use this
simulation data

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:58.790
to get near realtime sort of
what happens in the real world.

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:02.180
And the idea behind this is that
the red ball is the one doing

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:03.290
reinforcement learning.

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:04.880
So its sort of
objective is to try

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:08.180
to move toward that target
point, the yellow ball, which

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:09.202
is stationary.

00:17:09.202 --> 00:17:11.160
And it's trying to move
around and get to that.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.339
And then the blue
ball is our chaser,

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:16.400
sort of just doing a simple
scripted thing to directly

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:17.754
after the red ball.

00:17:17.754 --> 00:17:19.670
And the red ball is
trying to learn from that.

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:21.253
And the idea of this
is it's a "target

00:17:21.253 --> 00:17:23.660
point" and "point to avoid"
structure of initial model

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:25.970
simulations, that we
can create our chaser

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.970
and runner from the same model.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:32.480
And the idea from
our experimentation

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:35.030
led to this "peak
and valley" model,

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.940
what is more efficient and sort
of realistic for our runner

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:39.496
than other things we did.

00:17:39.496 --> 00:17:41.120
And so the idea behind
this is, imagine

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:45.510
that the blue ball is this
peak of a mountain which moves.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:48.650
And this yellow is this pit that
we can either keep stationary

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:50.810
or move around.

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:53.502
And red is sort of being
pulled towards the yellow pit.

00:17:53.502 --> 00:17:54.960
DAVE ELLIOTT: Kind
of like gravity.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:56.960
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah, gravity,
sort of falling away

00:17:56.960 --> 00:17:58.280
from the blue ball.

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:02.315
And this is sort of a common-ish
technique in robot pathfinding.

00:18:02.315 --> 00:18:04.190
And to make this a little
bit more realistic,

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:06.710
we also added a
aggressiveness rating.

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:09.230
And the idea behind this
was we would sort of weight

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:12.380
how much we wanted to
fall away from blue,

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:16.220
or sort of more
push into yellow.

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:19.340
And so this allowed us
to create two behaviors.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:22.930
When used as the chaser, you
can move the target point around

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:25.640
to the nearest Sphero robot that
you're trying to chase after,

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:28.370
and the nearest obstacle being
one of those white blocks

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:29.390
you want to avoid.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:30.890
And when the runner,
you can kind of

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:32.810
keep this stationary
general area

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:35.600
you want to move towards, with
the primary objective being

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:38.900
to run away from the nearest
blue ball or the nearest

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:39.484
chasing robot.

00:18:39.484 --> 00:18:40.941
DAVE ELLIOTT: And
so this is really

00:18:40.941 --> 00:18:43.280
the heart of the commander
model, which is really

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.760
the heart of the engine that
learned to play these games,

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:47.390
right?

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:47.930
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:49.305
DAVE ELLIOTT: And
on top of that,

00:18:49.305 --> 00:18:54.420
this also points out again
the example of-- or the use

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:57.050
of automation, that we
want to be able to--

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:02.714
if we'd have done this
in the real world,

00:19:02.714 --> 00:19:04.130
you need lots and
lots of examples

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:06.470
to be able to train your models.

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.810
This would have taken
weeks or months,

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:12.290
and cost hundreds
of thousands, maybe,

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:16.701
to have the many, many
iterations of this.

00:19:16.701 --> 00:19:18.200
What you've done
here is essentially

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.120
simulate those games.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:20.870
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:22.870
And one thing you might
be thinking is like, OK,

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:25.790
for chasing, why not just use
a search algorithm like A*

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:27.374
or Optimized.

00:19:27.374 --> 00:19:28.040
And that's true.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:28.850
You can totally do that.

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:30.433
We started with that
in our prototype,

00:19:30.433 --> 00:19:31.710
and it works really well.

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.437
But then when we wanted to make
that running away decision,

00:19:34.437 --> 00:19:36.020
you have to know
where you want to go.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.450
And that's that optimal
decision we don't exactly

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:42.380
know the answer to, where we
decided to create this model

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:44.520
and use it in both cases
of chasing and running,

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.390
which created some very
interesting behaviors we saw

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:49.540
out on the actual arena floor.

00:19:52.714 --> 00:19:54.130
So let's kind of
do a little recap

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:56.671
of all the different tools we
used in this training, and sort

00:19:56.671 --> 00:19:57.980
of decision-making process.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:00.880
So first up, going back to
that beginning, Cloud Storage,

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:03.520
using Google Cloud Storage
to hold our TensorFlow

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:07.660
record of 1,200 images
or so in our data set,

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:11.230
and slowly increasing throughout
time as we generate more data.

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:12.790
And then TensorFlow,
that machine

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:14.331
learning framework,
again, that we're

00:20:14.331 --> 00:20:17.380
using because the TensorFlow
object Detection API has

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:21.100
all this vast resources that
we have out there to use,

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:23.740
as well as the TensorFlow Lite,
which we'll be talking about

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.240
shortly.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:27.040
Cloud Machine Learning Engine--

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.530
we use that to run
many simulation jobs,

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:32.770
speed up our training
using GPUs and TPUs.

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:34.360
And the cool thing
with that is, by

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:37.060
doing that, we can speed up our
training to sort of cut down

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:39.997
our training time, from
running in about eight hours

00:20:39.997 --> 00:20:42.580
with Cloud TPU, down to about
an hour and a half to two hours,

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:44.729
that we can iterate multiple
times in a day instead

00:20:44.729 --> 00:20:45.520
of just once a day.

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:47.422
DAVE ELLIOTT: That's
from GPU to TPU.

00:20:47.422 --> 00:20:48.130
NOAH NEGREY: Yes.

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.260
DAVE ELLIOTT: And TPUs are the
TensorFlow Processing Units.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:52.540
These are custom-built
ASICs that are specifically

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:54.730
designed to run
in Google's Cloud

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.100
to do the types of
math that's required--

00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:00.400
the matrix type of math that's
required to do the training.

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:03.010
Training is the most intensive
part of the machine learning

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:06.010
process, at least from
a compute perspective.

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:08.800
And so having this
custom-built ASICs that really

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:13.342
increase the speed and
lower the cost of training

00:21:13.342 --> 00:21:14.800
is a really important
part of being

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.260
able to do this in a
quick and inexpensive way.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:18.010
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah.

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:20.593
And then we were able to use the
Google Compute engines to run

00:21:20.593 --> 00:21:23.230
our simulations on
that commander model,

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:25.300
by running multiple
simulations at once

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:27.610
so we aren't just focusing
all our resources locally.

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:33.870
So next up, let's talk about
the on-device deployment.

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:36.504
So we have our models
trained, selected,

00:21:36.504 --> 00:21:37.670
and we're ready to use them.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:41.420
We just need to put
them into action.

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:43.820
And so that's where we finally
get to TensorFlow Lite.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:45.600
And so TensorFlow
Lite is a solution

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:47.280
for running machine
learning models

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:49.950
on mobile and embedded devices.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:51.990
And the reason for
that is TensorFlow Lite

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:54.210
has this cool
quantization feature.

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:57.180
And the idea behind that is
that, for these deep learning

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:00.390
models, these models
that are very large,

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:04.110
you can shrink the model size
by removing certain layers.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.360
And you're trading
off accuracy to get

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.700
smaller models that run faster.

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:10.350
And this is really
important in mobile.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.900
And the reason for that
is mobile applications

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.910
are typically maybe
30 megabytes in size.

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:18.790
And if you add on a 30 megabyte
model size, that's a bad thing.

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:19.800
It doubles your size.

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:21.870
And I believe there's
requirements size

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:23.260
on these applications.

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:25.920
So by cutting them down
to about 2 megabytes,

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:29.340
you lose maybe a percent or two
of accuracy, but what you get

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:32.580
is a significantly faster
model, cutting, potentially,

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:34.890
your inference time--
your prediction time,

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.770
what it takes to do the
object detection-- in half

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.614
to a smaller model.

00:22:39.614 --> 00:22:41.280
And so this enables
the machine learning

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:44.040
inference with low
latency and a small binary

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.200
size, which can run
on Android, iOS,

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.950
and other operating systems.

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.660
And by converting
to TensorFlow Lite,

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:53.330
there's already existing
scripts to take your TensorFlow

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:55.917
model as is and convert
it to TensorFlow Lite.

00:22:55.917 --> 00:22:57.500
And so that's again
where we point out

00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:59.660
to that blog post of
training and serving

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:02.240
a realtime mobile
object detector in 30

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:03.660
minutes with Cloud TPUs.

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:05.974
It will help you run that
conversion script for you.

00:23:05.974 --> 00:23:07.890
DAVE ELLIOTT: And this
is another one of the--

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:09.250
as you mentioned
before, one of the key

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:10.850
reasons that we
chose TensorFlow.

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:13.900
Not only is TensorFlow
wildly popular,

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:15.350
so there's lots of resources--

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:18.080
I think it was open-sourced
in October of 2015,

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:24.260
and at this stage, it's by far
the most popular open source

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:26.390
library out there--
but also the fact

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:32.480
that TensorFlow Lite was
designed for use cases

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:33.190
just like this.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:33.898
NOAH NEGREY: Yes.

00:23:33.898 --> 00:23:36.420
Yeah, for mobile or
in embedded devices.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:38.440
So now we kind of have
this diagram flowing.

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.660
We have our training in the
cloud, those resources that we

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:42.284
went over, and then
we're converting it

00:23:42.284 --> 00:23:43.520
to TensorFlow Lite.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.870
Now we just need to put it
on the Android application.

00:23:46.870 --> 00:23:49.580
And so this we're going to be
serving on-device and talking

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:50.760
to our Sphero robots.

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:52.301
So we're talking to
the Sphero robots

00:23:52.301 --> 00:23:55.950
over their Bluetooth
with their SDK.

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:57.990
And so the idea of
this model deployment

00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:59.980
is there's sort of
a loop happening.

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:02.430
What happens is the
object detection model

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:03.690
informs the commander model.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:05.860
So when it goes and does
the object detection,

00:24:05.860 --> 00:24:08.140
it's understanding
where things are

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.840
and then sending
that information out

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:11.310
to the commander model.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:13.490
The commander model is then
taking that information

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:14.490
and making a prediction.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:17.040
It's saying, which angle
should I go, what direction.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.830
And the key thing is,
the Sphero robot's SDK

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:22.150
allows you to input an
angle of 360 degrees.

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:25.860
And our commander model
is making a decision

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:27.510
in 20-angle-degree increments.

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:30.070
So it's saying, cool, go
this way, go that way.

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:32.610
And so that information then
gets sent to our robots.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.620
And so then this
loop again repeats.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:36.430
We do the object
detection, we observe--

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:39.684
DAVE ELLIOTT: And the 20
angles was just arbitrary--

00:24:39.684 --> 00:24:41.350
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah,
relatively arbitrary.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.599
We just wanted to slim it
down because we didn't quite

00:24:43.599 --> 00:24:46.560
need full 360-degree support.

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:50.139
20-degree angles was enough
of a small percentage

00:24:50.139 --> 00:24:51.180
to get good behavior out.

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:53.550
DAVE ELLIOTT: Somebody else can
do it in different increments

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:54.550
if they need more precision.

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:55.070
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah.

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.147
DAVE ELLIOTT: Right, OK.

00:24:56.147 --> 00:24:58.230
NOAH NEGREY: Again, part
of the iterating and sort

00:24:58.230 --> 00:25:00.750
of process of trying
something, seeing

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:03.030
how it works once
it's out on the world,

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:05.140
and then bringing it back in.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:06.810
And so in general,
this whole loop

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:09.150
of running object detection
and commander model

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:10.860
takes about 60 to
80 milliseconds.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:13.920
That's the inference time
slash decision-making time.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:18.300
And that is near-ish realtime to
happening on the arena, where,

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:21.030
as the balls are moving, we
understand where they are,

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:22.920
as we saw in that
initial video--

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:25.590
where they're moving around,
and then making a decision based

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:26.424
on that information.

00:25:26.424 --> 00:25:28.589
DAVE ELLIOTT: So that's the
scene, the understanding

00:25:28.589 --> 00:25:30.430
and the reacting, 60
to 80 milliseconds.

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:31.150
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah, all of them.

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:33.120
DAVE ELLIOTT: Which
again, seems like it

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:37.234
would be fantastic in
the physical world.

00:25:37.234 --> 00:25:39.150
If it's an online game
or something like that,

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:40.810
that might be problematic.

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:44.040
But here, it's actually
seeing the direction

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:47.160
and the obstacles, and
making determinations

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:48.540
in 60-80 milliseconds.

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:49.290
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah.

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:55.370
So now we can see that whole
diagram coming together.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:25:57.500
But then the last
piece that's missing

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.140
is that multiplayer aspect
that we said, where people--

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:00.800
DAVE ELLIOTT: The humans.

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:03.383
NOAH NEGREY: --yeah, the humans
are coming in and interacting,

00:26:03.383 --> 00:26:05.250
and playing with
these AI robots.

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:09.410
So that's where we added this
second part using Firebase.

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:13.700
And Firebase is Google's sort
of mobile platform for cloud.

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:15.500
And the idea behind
this is we can

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:16.910
have our devices communicating.

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:19.610
And we use the Firebase
Realtime Database.

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:22.490
And the idea behind this is
that that realtime database

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:25.590
is holding our game state
and information of who

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:27.570
is "it," who is
allowed to move, if you

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:31.460
got tagged and are frozen, or
if you turned into a zombie.

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:36.080
And so we're using that realtime
database with low latency

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.920
to be able to update that game
state as things are happening,

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:41.510
so that the central
phone running the object

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:44.900
detection in commander model
is actually also running

00:26:44.900 --> 00:26:47.540
sort of the general game state,
and sending information off

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:48.680
to the Firebase.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:50.720
And the other devices
that the humans walk up to

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:52.940
and we start playing with
to interact with our robots

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:54.919
get that update in realtime.

00:26:54.919 --> 00:26:56.960
DAVE ELLIOTT: And in this
case, the human players

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.352
are using phones as well.

00:26:58.352 --> 00:26:59.060
NOAH NEGREY: Yes.

00:26:59.060 --> 00:27:00.643
DAVE ELLIOTT: With
a simple app on it.

00:27:00.643 --> 00:27:04.220
And they could've been using
joysticks or any other input

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:05.630
device.

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:07.734
And I guess it probably
makes sense to leave this

00:27:07.734 --> 00:27:08.900
up just for a minute longer.

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:12.380
Because this is really the
snapshot of the whole system.

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:15.417
On the far left side, you've
got the training in the cloud

00:27:15.417 --> 00:27:17.750
that I mentioned before as
one of the key tenets of what

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:18.950
we wanted to do.

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:20.750
You can see the storage
and the training

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:24.699
using TensorFlow, Google Cloud
Storage, Compute Engine, TPUs.

00:27:24.699 --> 00:27:26.990
The conversion-- that happens
in the middle, TensorFlow

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.665
and TensorFlow Lite.

00:27:28.665 --> 00:27:30.290
And then off to the
right side, serving

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:33.480
on-device with both of our
models, the object detection,

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.570
the scene, and the
commander models

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:39.530
are really the right model
for the right job at hand.

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:43.250
And then the Sphero
robots themselves,

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:46.100
that are both being
seen by object detection

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:49.430
and being controlled
through the commander model.

00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:51.390
And then the bottom
being just the gameplay,

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:53.760
the human interaction
component of it.

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:55.280
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah, exactly.

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:57.270
DAVE ELLIOTT: Great.

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:59.550
NOAH NEGREY: So let's jump
back to that initial video

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:02.460
and sort of walk through it
again now that we have it.

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:06.910
So the idea behind this is I am
the green ball, moving around.

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:09.744
I'm about to go and the AI bots
that are controlled elsewhere.

00:28:09.744 --> 00:28:12.160
And we're seeing that object
detection happen in realtime.

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.400
And so I've tagged
orange, pink, and red.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.691
And blue is still moving
around and making the decision

00:28:16.691 --> 00:28:19.260
to go untag its frozen
teammates and start

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:22.710
running away again, and then, as
it loops around that top block,

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:24.900
to go back and untag
the other teammates.

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:25.590
DAVE ELLIOTT: And
the white boxes

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:27.506
are just obstacles that
are [INAUDIBLE] scene,

00:28:27.506 --> 00:28:28.250
you try to avoid?

00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:30.750
NOAH NEGREY: Trying to add a
challenging factor to the game.

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:35.380
DAVE ELLIOTT: All right.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:40.700
So to summarize, we built
this system this year.

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:46.620
It wasn't a huge involvement
in terms of time or resources.

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:49.220
But it was a really fun project.

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:52.610
See, understand, and react
to the real world, leverage

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:57.030
existing resources to be
able to build this quickly.

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:59.180
The key things are
you're able to train

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:01.220
a complex model in the cloud.

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:02.980
Leverage all the
benefits of Cloud--

00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:05.020
since we're wearing
the T-shirts here--

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.100
but then deploy them
in the real world,

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:11.030
deploy them, in this case, on
just sort of fun Sphero robots

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:12.830
to play these fun little games.

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:17.270
But again, it could be
a robot in a warehouse,

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:20.270
or a manufacturing
plant, or in security,

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:24.509
or a whole host of
other use cases.

00:29:24.509 --> 00:29:26.300
The other thing I'll
point out, in summary,

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:30.130
is that all of these
tools are available today.

00:29:30.130 --> 00:29:32.750
TensorFlow and TensorFlow
Lite are open source.

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:36.870
Cloud machine Learning
Engine, GCS in the cloud

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:40.400
are easy to use and
relatively inexpensive to use.

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:41.660
You only pay for what you use.

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:44.740
In this case, part of
it is the training.

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:47.960
And all of these examples, all
these sample codes and blog

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:50.700
posts are really
readily available.

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:53.450
You can go out and use
them today, build them,

00:29:53.450 --> 00:29:57.470
play with them over the
holidays, and then modify them,

00:29:57.470 --> 00:29:59.050
and build something even better.

00:29:59.050 --> 00:29:59.090
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah.

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:01.160
And as we pointed out,
all of the resources,

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:03.260
specifically the
source code on that

00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:05.870
link we put at the beginning,
of the GitHub, Next 18

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:08.870
AI in Motion, has all
the source code links

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:11.060
to blog posts that go
even more in-depth,

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:14.190
as well as the other resources
that we used to build this.

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:15.940
So definitely go
and check it out.

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:16.940
DAVE ELLIOTT: OK, great.

00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:20.990
So please stay tuned
for live Q &amp; A.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:23.910
You can still enter
those questions,

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:26.360
and we'll be back in less
than a minute to answer

00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:27.367
your questions.

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:03.460
OK, welcome back.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:05.050
It looks like we've
got some questions

00:32:05.050 --> 00:32:07.930
from the live audience.

00:32:07.930 --> 00:32:10.900
Let's go ahead and start
with the first one.

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:13.600
Can you explain why you
use TensorFlow Lite?

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:17.800
So going back to what we
mentioned before, really it's

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:20.200
a question of, I
think, first, why

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:25.744
we want to use an embedded
solution in a mobile solution.

00:32:25.744 --> 00:32:27.160
That really goes
down to, again, I

00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:30.100
think, a really unique and
interesting emerging use case.

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:32.440
We're still really
early in the game.

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:36.640
As compute resources continue
to become less expensive,

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:41.740
as these mobile devices
become more and more powerful,

00:32:41.740 --> 00:32:43.960
we'll be able do more and
more of the inference, more

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:47.000
and more of the prediction
on these devices.

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:48.670
And I think that's
the first question,

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:50.253
is that there's
really interesting use

00:32:50.253 --> 00:32:54.109
cases that TensorFlow
Lite can address.

00:32:54.109 --> 00:32:55.900
And in terms of why we
use TensorFlow Lite,

00:32:55.900 --> 00:32:59.290
I think it's fair to say-- well,
Noah, you made that decision.

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:01.900
NOAH NEGREY: Yeah, so I
think it goes back to the--

00:33:01.900 --> 00:33:04.510
you can run TensorFlow and
TensorFlow Lite on Android.

00:33:04.510 --> 00:33:06.301
But the key difference
with TensorFlow Lite

00:33:06.301 --> 00:33:08.140
is you get that
quantization step

00:33:08.140 --> 00:33:10.660
in when you're speeding up
and shrinking your model size,

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:12.670
without losing much accuracy.

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:16.840
And so by doing that, you have
your low-latency fast model

00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:18.740
without even having
to go up to the cloud.

00:33:18.740 --> 00:33:20.860
So you've got your
training model on-device,

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:24.340
so you're not costing any
data going out to the cloud,

00:33:24.340 --> 00:33:29.929
and you're not incurring any
network latency cost usage.

00:33:29.929 --> 00:33:31.720
So that's sort of the
main key thing there,

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:34.480
is that you're keeping it
locally on-device for faster

00:33:34.480 --> 00:33:37.270
things, and you're not
costing your users data

00:33:37.270 --> 00:33:38.420
when they're running it.

00:33:38.420 --> 00:33:39.753
DAVE ELLIOTT: I think it's fair.

00:33:39.753 --> 00:33:42.640
I would also add to that that,
generally speaking, anything

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:45.820
you do in TensorFlow now is
becoming easier and easier

00:33:45.820 --> 00:33:49.990
because there's such a
robust ecosystem now.

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:52.700
There's so much
information out there

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:56.900
that can help you
do what we've done,

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:59.475
which is deliver
this pretty quickly.

00:33:59.475 --> 00:34:00.850
Second thing--
what's the biggest

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:02.760
obstacles in doing this?

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:04.436
Where did you hit
your roadblocks?

00:34:04.436 --> 00:34:07.210
Ooh, we hit a fair
number of roadblocks.

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:09.940
NOAH NEGREY: Well, I'd say
the first biggest obstacle

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:12.610
with machine learning in general
is sort of being overwhelmed

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:15.460
by either the task
and the problem

00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:17.050
or the amount of
resources out there,

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:18.710
and trying to sift through.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:21.340
And so I think that's the
initial burden, of like, oh,

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:23.380
wow, this is a huge space.

00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:24.610
It's daunting.

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:27.250
At least it was for me
when we started this.

00:34:27.250 --> 00:34:29.320
But going out, and sort
of digging through,

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:31.951
and finding these different
resources that we have,

00:34:31.951 --> 00:34:33.909
you can see, oh, wow,
they do a really good job

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:37.770
of explaining, walking
you through the steps,

00:34:37.770 --> 00:34:38.570
and so forth.

00:34:38.570 --> 00:34:40.870
I think the biggest
roadblock we really

00:34:40.870 --> 00:34:43.540
hit was actually with
our commander model.

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:46.030
And that was actually--
when we deploy it,

00:34:46.030 --> 00:34:48.550
we have our simulations,
they look great,

00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:51.310
but some of the behaviors
that we would then deploy,

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:53.350
we learned, oh dear,
this doesn't really

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:56.239
work in the real world, or
it learns a bad behavior.

00:34:56.239 --> 00:35:00.310
So if our reward system was
poor in our initial commander

00:35:00.310 --> 00:35:02.620
models, the Sphero
robot sort of just

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:06.130
learned, hey, if I go move to
the wall, and just stay there,

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:08.320
and don't move, that's the
best decision I can make,

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:10.236
because our reward system
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:35:10.236 --> 00:35:11.530
giving it back.

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:14.710
We gave it a poor reward
system where that was its best

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:16.360
score it could determine.

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:20.170
And so by modifying that, and
moving to that target and point

00:35:20.170 --> 00:35:24.280
thing, with the mountain
falling away and gravity,

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:27.550
we got a better simulation out,
which then also worked really

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:29.020
well in the real world.

00:35:29.020 --> 00:35:32.500
DAVE ELLIOTT: And I think the
an interesting thing on this

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:37.150
is I think some of the biggest
obstacles that you might hit

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:41.140
are, ironically, the
physical part of it--

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:49.900
the mat, the walls, the camera
being suspended onto the arena.

00:35:49.900 --> 00:35:53.620
None of it is
terribly difficult,

00:35:53.620 --> 00:35:55.840
but it does take some
effort to be able to set up

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:57.320
an environment like that.

00:35:57.320 --> 00:36:01.930
Initially, we used 2
by 4's and LEGO bricks

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:05.150
to suspend the camera in place.

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:07.984
And ultimately, what you
saw in the images earlier

00:36:07.984 --> 00:36:09.400
were things that
were put together

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:12.340
by an interactive
professional agency,

00:36:12.340 --> 00:36:15.040
hardened so they could
go around to trade shows.

00:36:15.040 --> 00:36:17.500
But that was actually
an interesting--

00:36:17.500 --> 00:36:19.100
the physical development
of it was one

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:20.350
of the interesting challenges.

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:22.808
NOAH NEGREY: But the videos
we're done with our prototype--

00:36:22.808 --> 00:36:24.860
2 by 4's, we went to
Home Depot, bought

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:28.000
some plywood, put
it together, done.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:30.880
DAVE ELLIOTT: $50 or
less worth of equipment.

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:34.300
All right, can you can apply
this to other common scenarios

00:36:34.300 --> 00:36:35.950
aside from gaming?

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:37.240
Yes, gaming is fun.

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:40.030
But as I mentioned
a few times, to me,

00:36:40.030 --> 00:36:48.040
one of the really most evident
use cases is in a warehouse,

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:49.280
in inventory management.

00:36:49.280 --> 00:36:52.360
You can set up simple cameras to
be able to look at a warehouse,

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:54.562
be able to track things
as they move around,

00:36:54.562 --> 00:36:56.770
to be able to see what's
happening in that warehouse.

00:36:56.770 --> 00:36:59.080
And then to be able to
understand that, oh, we're

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:02.380
short on this, we've got
an overabundance of that,

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:04.420
and then to make
decisions based upon what

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:07.120
it sees to be able to
take corrective action.

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:11.380
And then, ultimately, through
maybe warehouse robotics,

00:37:11.380 --> 00:37:14.650
to be able to take that
action, to go and automatically

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:16.450
order a resupply,
have it delivered,

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:17.950
and resupply those shelves.

00:37:17.950 --> 00:37:20.050
That's a simple
real world example.

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:21.758
NOAH NEGREY: On top
of that, you can also

00:37:21.758 --> 00:37:23.079
do some defect detection.

00:37:23.079 --> 00:37:25.120
So if you have an assembly
line sort of going by,

00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:27.036
and you're checking your
products for defects,

00:37:27.036 --> 00:37:30.070
you can go and monitor that as
well with this same technology.

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:33.160
We've done something else
with a similar thing,

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:35.920
using the same resources that
we had for object detection

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:39.520
to create something to detect
if there was a error in, say,

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:41.740
one of the objects moving
by, and say, hey, this

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:43.900
is a good object,
this is a bad one.

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:45.440
And then you can
use a second model

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:47.680
to make a decision
to react to that,

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:51.040
and either pull that object
aside, or make an alert

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:53.080
and have one of
your staff come up

00:37:53.080 --> 00:37:55.360
and grab it, move it, and
do some manual inspection

00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:56.330
from there.

00:37:56.330 --> 00:37:58.037
So there's a lot of
things you can do.

00:37:58.037 --> 00:38:00.370
It's just sort of coming up
with the idea of what to use

00:38:00.370 --> 00:38:01.780
this object detection for.

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:04.820
DAVE ELLIOTT: Well, I talk a
lot about machine learning.

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:08.770
And it's really gotten
to the stage where you

00:38:08.770 --> 00:38:12.100
need to think of the world as--

00:38:12.100 --> 00:38:15.490
you have systems now that
can understand the world,

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:17.860
whether it's understanding
somebody speak

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:22.900
or understanding what's
happening in a video

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:29.045
or in physical pictures,
it can see the world

00:38:29.045 --> 00:38:30.920
and understand what's
happening in the world.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:34.120
And once you realize that
you can take that and put it

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:37.750
on disconnected
mobile devices, I

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:40.167
think the litany of
things you can do really

00:38:40.167 --> 00:38:41.000
becomes interesting.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:42.583
And again, we're
really early in this,

00:38:42.583 --> 00:38:46.760
and this is just an easy way of
showing how this can be done.

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:49.430
I did want to touch on the
last question, which is,

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:51.920
can you review the
GitHub repositories?

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:54.730
So this should be in the
bottom of what you're

00:38:54.730 --> 00:38:57.530
looking at on your screen.

00:38:57.530 --> 00:39:00.890
I think we included just
about all of the links,

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:06.989
including the Google
Cloud Platform AI Next--

00:39:06.989 --> 00:39:08.155
NOAH NEGREY: The repository.

00:39:08.155 --> 00:39:08.620
DAVE ELLIOTT: --link.

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:09.650
NOAH NEGREY: That's
the main one that

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:11.858
will at least start getting
you out to the other ones

00:39:11.858 --> 00:39:14.710
that we used.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:17.112
DAVE ELLIOTT: So
hopefully we've answered

00:39:17.112 --> 00:39:18.070
